  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Equity futures shrugged off a hawkish Fed and ran with the AI story overnight: Nasdaq 100 futures added 1.3%, the KOSPI surged 3% and the Nikkei rose 1.1% after blowout Microsoft results eased fears that the AI capex boom isn't paying off. But the real action is at the long end of the curve. The Fed's divided hold on Wednesday — with commentary suggesting a hike may now be on the horizon — plus jobless claims at their lowest since mid-May has resurgent inflation worry pummelling duration: the Long Bond fell 1.1% and Ultras 1.3% while the two-year barely moved, a classic bear-steepener that signals skepticism the Fed will get ahead of prices.

The BoE reinforced the hawkish G7 tone, holding at 3.75% in a 6-3 split with three members voting for a hike and guidance it "stands ready to act," seeing inflation peaking at 3.2% in Q4. Geopolitics is the other axis. Fresh US strikes on Iran and a conflict spreading to Kuwait and Jordan — plus attacks on ships loading Kazakh crude at a Black Sea terminal — keep the supply-risk premium alive, though crude is consolidating its surge this morning: WTI -1.1% at $83.50, Brent under $90, products and TTF softer. That Black Sea escalation is bleeding into ags, with CBOT and KC wheat both up over 3% according to reports.

Copper's 2.2% pop reflects genuine market tightness meeting a still-uncertain Fed path, while gold at $4,136 is doing its job as the inflation-and-geopolitics hedge. Watch curve steepeners, Hormuz headlines and China's threatened retaliation over the US robot ban — trade friction is back on the tape.

